      "marks": 8,
      "answer": "$k=\\dfrac{1-e}{\\ln2}$ and, when $x=\\sqrt3$, $y=-\\dfrac12\\ln(2e-1)$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "Separate variables correctly. B1; $\\int\\frac{kx}{x^2+1}\\,dx=\\int e^{-2y}\\,dy$. Condone missing integral signs or missing $dx,dy$, but not both. Obtain a term $Ae^{-2y}$. B1 OE; $-\\frac12e^{-2y}$. Obtain a term $B\\ln(x^2+1)$. B1 OE; $\\frac{k}{2}\\ln(x^2+1)$. Obtain $-\\frac12e^{-2y}=\\frac{k}{2}\\ln(x^2+1)+C$. B1 OE. Use $y=0$ when $x=0$ to evaluate $C$, or use limits in a solution containing terms of the form $e^{-2y}$ and $\\ln(x^2+1)$. M1. Use $y=-\\frac12$ when $x=1$ to evaluate $k$, or use limits in such a solution. M1. Obtain $C=-\\frac12$ and $k=\\frac{1-e}{\\ln2}$. A1 OE; e.g. $-\\frac12e^{-2y}=\\frac{1-e}{2\\ln2}\\ln(x^2+1)-\\frac12$. Obtain final answer $y=-\\frac12\\ln(2e-1)$. A1, or exact equivalent. Total: [8]",
